<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amarapura</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amarapura is a former royal capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, now part of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>. It was founded by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a> in 1783 and served as capital from 1783 to 1821 and again from 1842 until 1859, when Mindon moved the court to Mandalay. It is known for the U Bein Bridge, a teak footbridge of about 1.2 kilometres across Taungthaman Lake, and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> weaving.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amarapura.jpg" alt="Amarapura" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mayor mt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmapura_City.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Amarapura comes from the Pali for The Immortal City. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a> of the Konbaung Dynasty founded the city as his new capital in May 1783, and it became a centre of Buddhist reforms and learning. In 1800 Buddhist clergy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> received higher ordination there and founded the Amarapura Nikaya, or Amarapura sect. In 1810 the town was estimated to hold 170,000 inhabitants, but a fire destroyed it that year. Bodawpaya's grandson, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagyidaw">Bagyidaw</a>, moved the court back to Ava in November 1821, and by 1827 the population of Amarapura was estimated at only 30,000.</p><p>King Tharrawaddy brought the royal capital back to Amarapura in February 1842. In February 1857 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">King Mindon</a> began to build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, 11 km to the north, as his new capital. The royal treasury had been depleted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-burmese-war">Second Anglo-Burmese War</a> of 1852, so Mindon reused as much material from Amarapura as possible. The palace buildings were dismantled and carried to the new site by elephant, and the city walls were pulled down for roads and railways. Amarapura officially ceased to be the capital on 23 May 1859, when Mandalay took over that role.</p><p>The ruins of the city wall show that it formed a square with sides of about three-quarters of a mile, and at each corner stood a solid brick pagoda about 30 m high. The most notable building was a temple adorned with 250 lofty pillars of gilt wood, which held a colossal bronze statue of the Buddha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Amarapura is now a township of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Irrawaddy River</a> on the west and the old capital site of Ava to the south. It is known for the Burmese tradition of acheik textile weaving, which began there and became popular under the Konbaung dynasty, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumptuary-law">sumptuary</a> laws regulated who could wear acheik clothing. It is also known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> weaving and bronze casting.</p><p>Its sights include the Pahtodawgyi, a stupa built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a> in 1816 outside the city walls, and the ruins of the Amarapura Palace, with the tombs of Bodawpaya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagyidaw">Bagyidaw</a>. The U Bein Bridge, a footbridge of about 1.2 km, was built by the mayor U Bein from teak columns salvaged from the old palace during the move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amarapura” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Konbaung kings ruled as Buddhist monarchs from palace cities laid out as cosmic centres, moving the capital several times, from Shwebo to Sagaing, Ava and <b>Amarapura</b>, and finally to Mandalay, founded by Mindon in 1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mahamuni image remained at Dhanyawadi until 1784, when the Konbaung dynasty carried it to <b>Amarapura</b>; its temple, now within Mandalay, is still a major pilgrimage site.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dhanyawadi">Kingdom of Dhanyawadi</a></li></ul></section>
